[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked **known: one issue, one fix queued**. It is not one issue.
Read report by report, the reports merged into it describe three different problems. The fix
that is queued, a day-first parser, closes only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Shipping the parser fix and closing #4471 leaves two problems open with no ticket of their own.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

Every count above is computed when the page renders, from the ticket export
(`reports-4471.json`), the tenant directory and the desk's notes (`desk-notes.json`). The
export repeats some reports across its pages; each report id counts once.

## The three problems

### `parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The parser reads `07/04` as July 4th, and the
booking moves to the mirrored date: *"Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July."* It shows in
every channel and at every tenant. This is the problem #4471 describes and the fix in the queue
is for.

### `hint`: booking-v3's form tells day-first people to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. The tenant's people do what the hint says,
and the form then reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The month flips just as with the
parser, so the reports read the same. But they come only through the form, only from tenants on
booking-v3, and the parser fix does not touch them: the hint is wrong, not the parser.

### `shift`: bookings move by one day, nothing is flipped

The desk read these reports as a one-day move and noted them, but filed them under `parser` by
tenant. They are not a flipped month: *"Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11
May."*, *"Every booking in June is one day off. Only June."*, *"Client in Lisbon got Tuesday.
Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting."*, *"The invite arrived before I made the booking."*
Same date, a different day for people in different time zones: a time-zone or
midnight-conversion bug. A day-first parser leaves every one of them as it is.

## Why they were merged

- Intake tags each report from the reporter's browser, and the tag is a guess. The table shows
  how often it disagrees with where the report belongs.
- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line alone, so
  nobody reads the body. *Wrong day on the invite* fits all three problems.
- `CauseTable` places a report by its tenant and channel. A one-day move from a day-first tenant
  lands under `parser` even when the desk has noted it reads differently.

## What changes

- Each problem has its own ticket, linked in the table above, with the reports it covers.
- The `parser` ticket is what #4471 was about. It closes when the parser fix ships.
- The `hint` and `shift` tickets stay open after that: neither closes with the parser fix.
- Read the body before you merge into a ticket marked known. A shared subject line is not a
  shared cause.

